# Motorola SM8635 device file contexts.
#
# UFS BSG (SCSI generic) character devices.
#
# qseecomd's gpt_init_service enumerates the UFS LUNs and reads each disk's GPT
# header through /dev/<host>:<channel>:<target>:<lun>. On arcfox those nodes are
# 0:0:0:0 .. 0:0:0:5. QCOM's sepolicy_vndr labels only two of them --
#
#   /dev/0:0:0:49476   u:object_r:vendor_bsg_device:s0          (the WLUN)
#   /dev/0:0:0:4       u:object_r:vendor_ufs_lun4_bsg_device:s0
#
# -- so 0, 1, 2, 3 and 5 fall through to the generic u:object_r:device:s0 and
# every read is denied. The boot log shows exactly that asymmetry: LUN 4 reads
# fine ("Successfully read 512 bytes from dev /dev/0:0:0:4") while all the
# others fail with errno 13, qseecomd exits 255, and with no TEE the keymint
# HAL never registers IKeyMintDevice -- which is what stalls keystore2, then
# vold, then the whole boot.
#
# tee already holds `allow tee vendor_bsg_device:chr_file rw_file_perms` in
# sm8650/generic/vendor/common/tee.te, so labelling is all that is needed here.
# The literal /dev/0:0:0:4 entry in QCOM's file_contexts still wins over this
# regex -- in file_contexts an exact path beats a pattern regardless of order.
/dev/0:0:0:[0-9]+               u:object_r:vendor_bsg_device:s0


# The FPC fingerprint HAL had NO label, so init refused to start it -- while
# sm8635-common/manifest.xml DECLARES android.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint.
# That is the same declared-but-absent trap that weaver just demonstrated: a
# system_server thread blocks in waitForDeclaredService forever. (It was not the
# thread that tripped the watchdog this time, but it is the next one in line.)
#
# Stock uses vendor_hal_fingerprint_fpc_exec, a Motorola type our policy does not
# define. hal_fingerprint_default_exec is AOSP's own type for an AIDL fingerprint
# HAL and does exist here, so use that; any extra access the FPC implementation
# needs will show up as denials rather than as a service that never runs.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.biometrics\.fingerprint-service\.fpc  u:object_r:hal_fingerprint_default_exec:s0

# See hal_sensorext.te: labelling the binary AND adding the service_contexts
# entry must happen together.
# Stock runs this binary in hal_graphics_composer_default, which already carries
# the /mnt/vendor/persist, display and sensor access it needs. Our bespoke
# vendor_hal_sensorext domain had only init_daemon_domain, and the service
# registered fine and then SIGABRTed on its first transaction inside
# SensorExt::initAlsComp reading /mnt/vendor/persist/sensors/als_* (84 crashes in
# 410s). No AVC was logged for vendor_hal_sensorext, so this is a stock-parity
# argument rather than a proven denial; if it still aborts, check whether those
# als_* files exist under /mnt/vendor/persist/sensors at all.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/motorola\.hardware\.sensorext-service  u:object_r:hal_graphics_composer_default_exec:s0

# MODEM EFS / FSG BLOCK DEVICES -- the RF calibration path.
#
# Every partition on this device fell through to the generic u:object_r:
# block_device:s0, so rmt_storage (which serves the modem's EFS over QMI) was
# denied its own storage:
#
#   avc: denied { read write } for comm="rmt_storage" name="sdd18"
#        scontext=u:r:vendor_rmt_storage:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:block_device:s0
#
# sdd18 is fsg. The consequence is not a log nuisance: with no EFS the modem has
# no RF calibration and REFUSES TO POWER THE RADIO ON, so there is no signal, no
# SIM, no SMS and no mobile data:
#
#   RILJ: RADIO_POWER error 71
#   CommandException: NO_RF_CALIBRATION_INFO
#   AnomalyReporter: "No RF calibration data"
#
# vendor_rmt_storage is already allowed rw on vendor_modem_efs_partition_device
# (sepolicy_vndr/legacy-um/generic/vendor/common/rmt_storage.te:51), so labelling
# is all that is needed. Paths and types taken verbatim from stock's
# /vendor/etc/selinux/vendor_file_contexts for the 1d84000.ufshc controller.
#
# THE SLOT-SUFFIXED PARTITIONS TAKE A DIFFERENT TYPE. This is the trap: stock
# carries BOTH an unsuffixed `fsg` entry AND an `fsg_[ab]` entry, and they are
# NOT the same type --
#   fsg        vendor_modem_efs_partition_device   (raw EFS, served by rmt_storage)
#   fsg_[ab]   vendor_modem_block_device           (an ext4 filesystem Android mounts)
# Labelling fsg_a as modem_efs (which this file did on the first attempt) denies
# e2fsck, and the boot log shows fs_mgr find the filesystem unclean, unmount it
# for a check, and the check fail:
#   e2fsck: Permission denied while trying to open .../by-name/fsg_a
# The whole set below is taken verbatim from stock's own vendor_file_contexts for
# this controller rather than extrapolated -- extrapolating the suffix is exactly
# what got it wrong.
#
# modemst1, modemst2 and fsc are genuinely not slot-specific and keep the EFS
# type. Verified live: with these, rmt_storage's denial disappears and it serves
# /boot/modem_fs1 and /boot/modem_fs2 from a cold boot with zero denials.
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/modemst1        u:object_r:vendor_modem_efs_partition_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/modemst2        u:object_r:vendor_modem_efs_partition_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/fsc             u:object_r:vendor_modem_efs_partition_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/fsg             u:object_r:vendor_modem_efs_partition_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/fsg_[ab]        u:object_r:vendor_modem_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/modem_[ab]      u:object_r:vendor_modem_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/bluetooth_[ab]  u:object_r:vendor_modem_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/ssd             u:object_r:vendor_ssd_block_device:s0
/dev/block/platform/soc/1d84000\.ufshc/by-name/persist         u:object_r:vendor_persist_block_device:s0
